winewayland: fix the pen's unconditional motion clamp; share one clamp with the pointer

wayland_tablet_tool_motion() pinned pen coordinates to the focused window's
rect however far outside it the pen actually was. The pen has the same implicit
grab as the mouse: while the tip is down, focus stays on the surface that
received the contact and the compositor keeps reporting motion well outside it.
Pinning those coordinates makes Wine believe the pen never left the source
window, so any pen drag that leaves its surface -- out of a popup, off a panel
-- never reaches a drop target.

This is the identical defect fixed for the mouse in 11.10-50 (Premiere's Button
Editor drag). The tablet copy was left on the old behaviour deliberately so
that test stayed clean, and then not revisited; it sat open for two weeks. Its
own comment said "// TODO: Copied directly from wayland_pointer."

So this does not copy the fix a third time. The clamp is now ONE function,
wayland_clamp_rounding_overshoot(), called by both wayland_pointer.c and
wayland_tablet.c, with the rationale at the single definition. Copying it is
exactly how the pen was missed.

The clamp itself is upstream and unconditional -- verified against pristine
wine 11.10, dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c:151-154 -- so both halves
are upstream-worthy.

Regression risk here is the MOUSE path, which was working and which this
refactors. Proven semantics-preserving rather than assumed: the pre-refactor
inline block (from the mkpatch snapshot) and the extracted helper were compiled
side by side and run over 2,064,422 points straddling every edge of four rect
shapes by -8..+8 px. Zero differences. Of the outside-the-rect cases, 42,354
pass through and 13,304 are clamped, so the grid genuinely exercises both arms.

⚠️ The PEN behaviour itself is unverified -- it needs the tablet.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
@@ -476,6 +476,13 @@
 void wayland_surface_coords_to_window(struct wayland_surface *surface,
                                       double surface_x, double surface_y,
                                       int *window_x, int *window_y);
+/* NEUTRON: bring a surface-derived screen point back inside `window_rect`, but ONLY when it is
+ * outside by a rounding-sized amount. Shared by the pointer and the tablet/pen: both derive a
+ * screen point the same way and both must clamp the same way. The reasoning, and why an
+ * UNCONDITIONAL clamp breaks every drag that leaves its source surface, is at the definition in
+ * wayland_pointer.c. ⛔ Do not re-inline this a third time -- the tablet copy is exactly how the
+ * 11.10-50 pointer fix missed the pen for two weeks. */
+void wayland_clamp_rounding_overshoot(POINT *screen, const RECT *window_rect);
 struct wayland_client_surface *wayland_client_surface_create(HWND hwnd);
 void wayland_client_surface_attach(struct wayland_client_surface *client, HWND toplevel);
 /* NEUTRON round-36 temporal suppression: record GDI damage flushed into a
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c
@@ -132,6 +132,55 @@
  * pointer_handle_motion_internal(). See the comment at the clamp below. */
 #define NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS 2
 
+/* Sometimes, due to rounding, we may end up with pointer coordinates slightly outside the target
+ * window, so bring them within bounds.
+ *
+ * 🚨 NEUTRON: clamp ONLY a ROUNDING-SIZED overshoot, never an arbitrary one. The clamp is
+ * upstream and its comment says "slightly outside", but it was unconditional: motion ANY distance
+ * outside the focused window was pinned to that window's edge. Wayland has an IMPLICIT GRAB --
+ * while a button (or the pen tip) is held, focus stays on the surface that received the press and
+ * the compositor keeps reporting motion far outside it. Pinning those coordinates makes Wine
+ * believe the cursor never left the source window, so EVERY drag that starts in a popup or
+ * secondary surface dies: the drop target never sees the pointer. Measured symptom (2026-08-12):
+ * Premiere's Program Monitor "Button Editor" highlights on hover but a button cannot be dragged
+ * onto the button bar.
+ *
+ * Drags entirely WITHIN one toplevel (project bin -> timeline) share a surface, never leave
+ * window_rect, and were never affected -- which is why this survived so long.
+ *
+ * The epsilon must cover fractional-scale rounding: coords_to_window() multiplies by
+ * surface->window.scale, so a 0.5 surface-px rounding error is up to scale/2 window px (~1.13 at
+ * the 2.25 scale a build tester runs). 2 px covers it with margin; anything beyond that is a real
+ * position and MUST be passed through so win32u can route it to the capture window.
+ *
+ * ⛔ SHARED WITH THE TABLET/PEN ON PURPOSE. This lived inline here and was copied verbatim into
+ * wayland_tablet_tool_motion() (its own comment said "Copied directly from wayland_pointer"). The
+ * 11.10-50 fix went into this copy only, so the pen kept the unconditional clamp and pen drags out
+ * of a popup kept failing for two weeks. One definition now; do not re-inline it. */
+void wayland_clamp_rounding_overshoot(POINT *screen, const RECT *window_rect)
+{
+    if (screen->x >= window_rect->right)
+    {
+        if (screen->x - window_rect->right < NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS)
+            screen->x = window_rect->right - 1;
+    }
+    else if (screen->x < window_rect->left)
+    {
+        if (window_rect->left - screen->x <= NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS)
+            screen->x = window_rect->left;
+    }
+    if (screen->y >= window_rect->bottom)
+    {
+        if (screen->y - window_rect->bottom < NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS)
+            screen->y = window_rect->bottom - 1;
+    }
+    else if (screen->y < window_rect->top)
+    {
+        if (window_rect->top - screen->y <= NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS)
+            screen->y = window_rect->top;
+    }
+}
+
 static void pointer_handle_motion_internal(wl_fixed_t sx, wl_fixed_t sy)
 {
     INPUT input = {0};
@@ -157,50 +206,7 @@
                                      (int *)&screen.x, (int *)&screen.y);
     screen.x += window_rect->left;
     screen.y += window_rect->top;
